\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{Overview of our evaluation framework. Given $N$ candidate LLM judges, we construct $K$-judge panels (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), subject them to six adversarial attack types (\S\ref{sec:threat_model}), and assess vulnerability through two complementary metrics: $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link individual susceptibility) and $K_{\text{eff}}$ (effective independent channels via mutual information). Panel decisions are aggregated through four methods of increasing sophistication (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}).}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

Multi-judge panels have emerged as the dominant defense against bias and manipulation in LLM-based evaluation. After \citet{zheng2023judging} demonstrated that individual LLM judges can rival human annotators, subsequent work exposed systematic vulnerabilities: position bias, verbosity preferences, and sycophancy compromise the reliability of any single judge \citep{wang2023fair}. The natural response has been to aggregate decisions across diverse models, drawing inspiration from Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT), where majority voting among independent nodes tolerates faulty responses \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}. A recent systematization of LLM-judge security reports that 7-model committees reduce attack success rates from 50--68\% to 10--19\% \citep{almasoud2026security}. Diversity, by this logic, is the key ingredient for secure evaluation.

Yet the evidence for diversity as a causal defense is weaker than it appears. The observed ASR reduction conflates panel diversity (judge independence) with individual robustness (strongest members). No existing work disentangles these across panel sizes. BFT adversaries control faulty nodes internally, whereas evaluation adversaries manipulate inputs externally; the SDPI bounds joint failure but assumes non-adaptive faults \citep{schneier2000secrets}.

In our experiments, we discover a panel-size-dependent vulnerability regime transition. At $K{=}3$, $\eta_{\max}$ dominates; at $K{\geq}5$, this reverses ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominant, $\eta_{\max}$ non-significant). At the aggregate level, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is positively associated with vulnerability in both regimes, consistent with one possible mechanism where independent judges present more targetable attack surfaces \citep{goodfellow2015adversarial,papernot2016transferability}. However, this pattern is attack-mechanism-dependent: GAM analysis shows higher $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ associated with \emph{increased} vulnerability for targeted attacks but \emph{decreased} vulnerability for shared-bias attacks, and Bayesian calibration reverses the direction entirely.

Controlled experiments across 15 models, $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, 6 attacks, and 2 benchmarks confirm this transition (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}). Wild cluster bootstrap shows $\eta_{\max}$: 5/12 $\to$ 0/12 $\to$ 0/12 across $K$, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: 1/12 $\to$ 7/12 $\to$ 5/12. Under random faults, panel composition is highly predictive ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$); the predictability collapse under adaptive attack is most pronounced at $K{=}3$. GAM reveals a 6:6 split by attack mechanism at $K{=}3$, largely preserved at $K{=}5$. Bayesian calibration reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction at $K{=}3$ and neutralizes both predictors at $K{=}5$; robustness-first selection dominates diversity-maximizing strategies.

These findings yield three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{K-dependent regime transition.} $\eta_{\max}$ dominates at $K{=}3$ (bootstrap 5/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 1/12, MDE${=}0.14$); reversal at $K{\geq}5$ ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 7/12, $\eta_{\max}$ 0/12). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient positive at aggregate level but direction depends on attack mechanism and aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:dominance}, \S\ref{sec:cross_k}).
\item \textbf{Adaptive-vs-random fault contrast.} Under random faults, $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$ surviving all corrections; the predictability collapse under adaptive attack is most pronounced at $K{=}3$ (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}).
\item \textbf{Attack-type taxonomy and aggregation.} 6:6 split by attack mechanism at $K{=}3$ (targeted vs.\ shared-bias), 11/12 consistent at $K{=}5$. Bayesian calibration reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction at $K{=}3$ and neutralizes both predictors at $K{=}5$ (\S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}, \S\ref{sec:aggregation}).
\end{enumerate}

\appendix

\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}}
\label{app:proof}

The replaced judge $J_K'$ is \emph{pivotal} exactly when $t{-}1$ of the remaining $K{-}1$ judges have already failed. Formally, let $S = \sum_{j=1}^{K-1} X_j^a$ denote the number of failures among the shared judges. The panel fails if and only if the $K$-th judge also fails when $S = t{-}1$, or when $S \geq t$ regardless of the $K$-th judge:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a) &= \Pr(S \geq t) + \Pr(S = t{-}1) \cdot \Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \mid S = t{-}1).
\end{align}
Since $\Pr(X_{K'}^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K}) \geq \Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K})$ for all attacks $a$ and realizations $\mathbf{x}_{-K}$ (by hypothesis), the second term is weakly larger for $\mathcal{P}'$ than for $\mathcal{P}$ under every attack. Taking the supremum over attacks preserves the inequality: $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}') \geq \mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P})$. The result holds under arbitrary inter-judge correlations and any attack space $\mathcal{A}$.

\section{Robustness Correction Methods}
\label{app:robustness_methods}

The 455 panels are constructed combinatorially from 15 shared judges: each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, inducing correlated residuals across panel-level observations. Standard OLS assumes independent errors, potentially inflating significance. We apply three corrections.

\paragraph{Clustered Standard Errors.} Because each panel of $K{=}3$ judges does not map to a single cluster, we perform three separate one-way clusterings (one for each judge position in the ordered panel tuple) using CR1 bias-corrected sandwich estimators (13 clusters per position). The reported standard error for each coefficient is the maximum across the three clusterings, yielding the most conservative inference.

\paragraph{Mixed Effects.} For each of the three judge positions, we fit a linear mixed-effects model with a random intercept for the judge occupying that position, estimating $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ as fixed effects. The reported $p$-value for each coefficient is the maximum across the three models.

\paragraph{Wild Cluster Bootstrap.} We perform $B{=}9{,}999$ bootstrap resamples using Rademacher weights, clustering by each of the three panel positions in turn and taking the maximum $p$-value across positions (the most conservative aggregation). This approach avoids distributional assumptions on the cluster structure.

\section{Extended Analysis}
\label{app:extended_analysis}

\subsection{BFT Reversal Mechanism: Detailed Evidence}
\label{app:bft_reversal}

Under standard OLS, the regression results (Table~\ref{tab:dominance}) show $\eta_{\max}$ significant in 11 of 12 conditions and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ positively associated with ASR in 7. Non-independence correction reveals an asymmetry: $\eta_{\max}$ is significant in 5 to 8 of 12 conditions depending on correction method (clustered SE: 8/12; mixed effects: 7/12; wild cluster bootstrap: 5/12), while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses to 0--1/12 (clustered SE 0/12; bootstrap 1/12; mixed effects 3/12). The directional pattern (point estimates predominantly positive) persists, but statistical confidence is limited. The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) sharpens this: under random faults, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s positive association with panel error survives all corrections (2/2 conditions, $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), indicating a genuine relationship that becomes artifactual under adaptive attack (0--1/12 after correction).

Under OLS, the sole negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient is ARC $\times$ sycophancy ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$, FDR $p = 0.013$), though this effect does not survive non-independence correction (clustered $p = 0.441$). The GAM taxonomy subsumes this individual exception: the directional pattern across all 6 shared-bias conditions is the stronger evidence, not any single condition's significance.

\subsection{Diversity--Capability Tradeoff}
\label{app:diversity_capability}

The $r = 0.70$ correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ reflects a structural feature of the current LLM ecosystem: maximizing panel diversity requires sampling across capability tiers, which systematically introduces less capable and therefore more vulnerable judges. The structural correlation is unaffected by SE correction; it is a property of the model ecosystem, not an artifact of panel overlap. However, the high confound combined with fragile independent significance means that observational regression cannot reliably disentangle diversity's causal contribution from the capability deficit that co-occurs with it.

Family-level labels provide indirect evidence that capability proximity matters more than taxonomic grouping: family membership is not a significant predictor of panel vulnerability ($p = 0.179$ on MMLU, $p = 0.385$ on ARC), consistent with models from the same provider exhibiting heterogeneous vulnerability profiles that vary by capability tier rather than by shared architecture \citep{kuncheva2003measures}.

\subsection{Aggregation Mechanism Details}
\label{app:aggregation_details}

Bayesian calibration flips the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient from predominantly positive under majority vote to predominantly negative (6 of 7 significant coefficients negative). The mechanism is calibrated reliability weighting: by estimating each judge's confusion matrix on clean data, Bayesian aggregation downweights weak judges, effectively implementing robustness screening within the aggregation rule itself. Threshold voting amplifies both channels because any single dissenting judge vetoes the panel decision, making compositional factors almost perfectly predictive of attack success ($R^2{=}0.89$). Bayesian calibration requires a clean accuracy prior, and an adaptive attacker aware of this calibration could target the prior itself \citep{tambe2011security}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_aggregation_gradient.pdf}
\caption{Distribution of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients across aggregation methods. Bayesian aggregation reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction (1 positive, 6 negative), contrasting sharply with majority voting (7 positive, 1 negative).}
\label{fig:aggregation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Metric Robustness Under Partial Panel Information}
\label{app:oracle}

The main analysis computes $\eta_{\max}$ with full knowledge of panel composition (all three judges known). In deployment, a practitioner selecting or auditing a panel may know only a subset of the constituent judges. We test whether $\eta_{\max}$ retains predictive power under such partial information by recomputing it from two of the three panel members: for each panel, we cycle through all $\binom{3}{1} = 3$ leave-one-out masks, compute $\eta_{\max}$ from the two known judges, and average the regression estimates across masking scenarios.

Under the most conservative inference (partial information + clustered SE), $\eta_{\max}$ remains the sole significant predictor with $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.589$, achieving significance in 12/12 conditions, stronger than the oracle baseline (8/12 under clustered SE). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ loses all predictive power (0/12). The averaging over masking scenarios smooths estimation noise inherent in the three-judge oracle computation, strengthening the $\eta_{\max}$ signal. This demonstrates that $\eta_{\max}$ computed from partial panel information is robust to incomplete composition knowledge and retains (indeed improves) its predictive power for panel ASR.

\section{Panel Selection Strategy Comparison}
\label{app:panel_selection}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_panel_design.pdf}
\caption{Panel attack success rate under three selection strategies. $\min(\eta_{\max})$ achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions, confirming that robustness-first selection dominates diversity-maximizing strategies.}
\label{fig:panel_design}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:panel_design} compares three panel selection strategies: $\min(\eta_{\max})$ (select the panel minimizing weakest-judge vulnerability), random selection, and $\max(K_{\mathrm{eff}})$ (maximize diversity). The $\min(\eta_{\max})$ strategy achieves the lowest ASR in all 12 conditions, consistent with the weakest-link dominance documented in \S\ref{sec:dominance}.

\section{Forest Plot: Coefficient Estimates with Clustered Standard Errors}
\label{app:forest_plot}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Standardized coefficients with clustered SE 95\% CIs for $\eta_{\max}$ (left) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (right) across 12 conditions. Filled circles: FDR $< 0.05$. $\eta_{\max}$ shows consistent positive effects (8/12 significant); $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ CIs predominantly contain zero (0/12 significant).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation.}
LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes (position bias, verbosity, sycophancy), motivating multi-judge panels; \citet{shi2024judgerobust} further quantified robustness gaps under adversarial prompts. Aggregating noisy annotators traces back to \citet{dawid1979maximum}; \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias driving within-family correlations. These frameworks assume benign conditions; we test whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation. \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\%, but did not disentangle diversity from individual robustness.

\paragraph{Condorcet Jury Theorem.}
\citet{condorcet1785essai} proved that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5. \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended the theorem to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee; \citet{kaniovski2011optimal} derived optimal jury size under correlated votes. LLM judge outputs exhibit precisely this correlation structure, and our results instantiate the CJT failure mode: when adaptive attacks push judges below the critical threshold, panel expansion amplifies error.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity Theory.}
\citet{krogh1995neural} proved that ensemble error equals average member error minus a diversity term; \citet{brown2005managing} showed diversity reduces error only when members maintain sufficiently low bias. In our setting, weaker judges introduce bias large enough to offset diversity benefits. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} found that no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicted ensemble improvement; our $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a $K$-dependent pattern: at $K{=}3$, OLS significance collapses after correction (8/12$\to$1/12); at $K{\geq}5$, it survives bootstrap inference (7/12). Recent information-theoretic frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness and Transferability.}
Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. \citet{tramer2018ensemble} introduced ensemble adversarial training but did not eliminate cross-model transfer. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving} or diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision, but requires coordinating member training---a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection.

\paragraph{BFT and Security Games.}
Classical BFT \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical} tolerates up to $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle that an attacker exploits the single most vulnerable component. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate BFT's independence assumption, motivating our empirical decomposition.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Evaluation.}
Information-theoretic approaches to LLM evaluation address statistical validity \citep{robertson2025stepwise,feng2026noisy}, single-model attack costs \citep{kaneko2025bits}, sequential selection \citep{jung2026logarithmic}, and truthfulness \citep{chen2025incentivizing,qiu2026truthfulness}, but assume honest judges or single-model adversaries. We address the remaining gap: adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

